#X text 20 20 u_map -- the map \, and the only file in Cut It that says what a control means. Devices publish named controls on param \; this turns them into the buses and the output devices' cords \, so nothing in e_ or c_ learns that a nanoKONTROL exists. ref/module/map.md, f 58;
#X text 20 165 creation arg 1: the state directory \, the \$3 u_state takes: the recover breadcrumb lives beside the state files. inlet 1: a BPM a tap or the phone set \, from u_tempo: knob 1 is held there until turned through it. outlet 1: the Volca \, selector-prefixed -- notes <n> <v> <d> \, cc <c> <v> \, program <n> \, note <n> <v>. One outlet per output device \, wired in u_root (C-2): the SP-404's went when it left the rig (2026-11-04). outlet 2: c_steps \, what a press on the drum page means -- step \, vel or pad \, <n> <velocity> \, and a drum-page pad's release at velocity 0 -- and the drums destination's <arg> <0-1> \, knobs 2 to 4's last \, track-last and swing. outlet 3: u_net \, a bang that flips test mode. outlet 4: c_synth \, synth <choice> \, a page-3 pad's press. outlet 5: u_tempo \, what a press of a page-1 pad means \, its role in cut-it-arrangement-page.txt: tap. outlet 6: u_song \, the top row's top <1-8> \, and save and revert., f 58;
#X text 700 20 The map is a table \, and mode-dependent: cut-it-map.txt \, one row per mapping \, four atoms always -- <mode> <control> <dest> <arg>. u_map-lua reads it and looks each control up., f 76;
#X text 1400 20 The allowlist guard is what makes a table acceptable: a row names a destination that must be a literal argument on the route below \, feeding a handler on this canvas \, and never a send. Skip it and nothing fails., f 76;
#X text 2200 20 A row naming a destination not on the route is a real error \, unknown-dest on err. An unmapped control is normal: it is silent on every bus \, and draws nothing (Brendan \, 2026-11-04)., f 62;
#X text 20 1700 The eight mode keys stay hardcoded and come first \, so a page change never depends on the page and a broken table cannot strand you. They are the Launchpad's left column \, CC 80 down to 10 \, which g_grid lights as the mode lamps. See the select 0 gates beside them., f 62;
#X text 1080 1700 The lookup is Lua: u_map-lua reads cut-it-map.txt when it is made \, keeps the key's mode \, divides each value into 0-1 \, holds a restored knob until it crosses (pickup) \, and looks the control up. A mapped control leaves as five atoms for the route below \; an unmapped one leaves by no outlet \, since only a control the map binds names itself. The rules are u_map.lua \, tested without Pd by test/gate/lua/u_map_test.lua (ref/module/map.md)., f 62;
#X text 2140 1700 C-6: the Lua sends the looked-up row as a list \, and route matches a selector \, so without the list trim every control would take the reject as unknown-dest., f 62;
#X text 20 1930 At load the Lua answers init with a bang on its last outlet \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded -- pdlua missing \, or an error in the Lua -- and fail u_map no-lua says so on the screen: every control is dead but the eight mode keys., f 62;
#X text 1080 1930 Pickup's rules are the Lua's and its clock and files are here: the boot window shuts at 1000 ms \, and the breadcrumb at 2000 says live when the boot follows a recover. Whether a Save left a knobs.txt is the Lua's \, asked as it is made (items 236 \, 239 and 299)., f 62;
#X text 1080 2160 Arg then value \, always: unpack fires right to left \, so the value is in a cold inlet or a gate before the arg triggers., f 62;
#X text 2140 2160 Value destinations are c_mapped instances \, and the scaling lives there: tempo is c_mapped bpm 990 10 \, 0-1 over 10 to 500 BPM. Each draws its own row and passes arg and scaled value on only while live. The transport chain holds no state: it reads back r start and r stop., f 62;
#X text 20 2390 The spigot is the trigger test \, the same three boxes everywhere: a press is non-zero after normalizing and a release is 0 \, so one test serves every surface., f 62;
#X text 1080 2390 The Volca handlers build the selector-prefixed message m_volca expects. C-6, f 62;
#X text 2140 2390 volca-prog uses the value as a gate \, since the program number is the arg: a fader release cannot select a patch., f 62;
#X text 20 2850 The seed fills a silence and sets no default: nothing else writes mode at load \, and any incoming mode closes the spigot., f 62;
#X text 1080 2850 u_map is a state contributor: mode is an auto key. The table itself is not persisted \, as it is code. The Lua puts a mode in the store only when its key took it \, so a bad mode cannot survive a power cycle (item 297)., f 62;
#X text 2140 2850 A restore arrives as a real selection: route restore then route mode leaves the page \, mode-1 \, as its selector \, the shape s mode wants. A mode the old code saved \, compose or perform and then the page \, loses its class at route compose perform and comes back as its page alone. It lands at about 3.5 s \, after the seed's spigot has shut., f 62;
#X obj 720 440 loadbang;
#X obj 720 490 del 1000;
#X msg 720 580 window-shut;
#X text 2140 1930 The screen shows the mapped value \, named for the destination (bpm 157 \, volca-cc41 64) \, not the raw position (item 238) \, and an unmapped control nothing. volca-key and the button destinations draw nothing either (ref/module/map.md)., f 62;
#X obj 3400 3620 loadbang;
